 "title": "Battle of Dien Bien Phu",
 "date_label": "13 March - 7 May 1954",
 "year": 1954,
 "intro": "In November 1953 French commander Navarre parachuted troops into the remote Muong Thanh valley in\n            north-west Vietnam, planning a fortified base that would lure the Viet Minh into a pitched battle and\n            shield Laos. The garrison grew to roughly 10,800 men and was supplied only by air. General Vo Nguyen Giap\n            encircled it with about 50,000 troops and hauled artillery, including anti-aircraft guns and howitzers,\n            over the forested hills. After abandoning an early quick-strike plan, he opened the assault on 13 March\n            1954 and, step by step, took the outlying strongpoints, then closed in with trenches until the last\n            positions fell on 7 May. The surrender ended the French war in Indochina.",
 "outcome": "Viet Minh victory: the French garrison surrendered, about 11,700 were captured, and the First Indochina War ended in French withdrawal.",
 "uncertainty": "Exact strongpoint and headquarters positions, the artillery sites, and the paths of the Viet Minh divisions are reconstructed from the usual maps and marked conjectural. Casualty figures vary widely between Western, Asian and Vietnamese sources. The alleged confrontation of de Castries by Langlais on 24 March is disputed by some historians. Unit sizes are schematic and not drawn to strength.",
